{"kind":"request","text":"codex: hugh's fourth layering finding. The identity mutation surface can be driven but not observed, and half its lifecycle is missing.\n\nWHAT I VERIFIED. chess.go:974 wraps identity.Endorse. chess.go:779 Decision says in its own comment that unknown and host records are not chess decisions and returns found=false for them. cmd/chess/main.go:572 actionResult builds its result from exactly that call, so an anchor — which produces a host record — comes back with a decision chess declines to make. The MCP tool list at main.go:416 offers anchor and nothing else: no revoke, no query. And identity.Endorse at host/identity/record.go:49 deliberately records endorsements whose signer has no standing, which is the right design for a log but means writing succeeded tells you nothing about whether authority was created.\n\nWHY THIS MATTERS MORE THAN IT LOOKS. Put together, an agent can call anchor, get no error, and have no way to learn whether it just granted seat-recovery authority or wrote an inert record. It cannot list what authority stands. It cannot take authority back. The one operation that is exposed is the one that grants power, and the two that would let someone check or undo it are absent. That asymmetry is the problem: a surface where the only available action is to grant is worse than no surface, because it invites use without the means to audit or reverse.\n\nTHE PROPERTY I DO NOT WANT LOST. Endorse accepting a standing-less signer is correct and must stay. A log that refuses records it disagrees with hides the disagreement; recording the endorsement and judging it separately is the whole design. So this is not a request to validate harder at the boundary. It is a request to make the judgment legible to the caller. Written and inert must be distinguishable from written and effective, and right now they are not.\n\nON THE DECISION API. I am not telling you which way to fix the reporting, because both readings are defensible. Widening chess Decision to cover host records makes chess speak about records it does not judge, which may be exactly wrong. Having the anchor path report the host record's own outcome keeps that boundary but needs the anchor path to know something it currently does not ask. Your call; say which you chose and why in the artifact, since the reason matters more than the mechanism here.\n\nTHE FAILURE I WANT THE TESTS TO CATCH. The easy wrong fix is to report success whenever the append succeeded, which would be true, useless, and indistinguishable from the correct behaviour in every passing test. That is why the conditions ask you to make the reporting path return success unconditionally and confirm a named assertion fails. If that mutation stays green, the tests are describing the code rather than constraining it.","body":{"conditions":"Not urgent; sequenced behind the display-names repair, the playability design, the binding-recovery CLI and the identity-threshold lane. Make the identity mutation surface report its own outcome and complete its lifecycle. First, an anchor performed through chess must tell the caller whether recovery authority was actually created, rather than reporting a null decision because the record is a host record and chess Decision returns found=false for those. Decide deliberately whether the fix is to widen what chess reports on, or to have the anchor path report the host record's own outcome without pretending it is a chess judgment; either is acceptable, but the caller must be able to distinguish created from refused from unknown, and the distinction must be honest rather than assumed from the absence of an error. Second, add the missing halves of the lifecycle: a way to see what anchors currently stand for a subject or scope, and a way to revoke a delegation, both through the shipped interface rather than only through host. Preserve the existing property that identity.Endorse records an endorsement whose signer has no standing, since refusing those at the boundary would hide them from the log; the requirement is that a caller can tell such an endorsement carries no authority, not that it cannot be written. Regression tests written from the desired behaviour: an anchor that creates authority is reported as effective, an endorsement by a signer without standing is reported as carrying no authority rather than as success, a query lists a standing anchor and stops listing it after revocation, and a revocation by an actor without authority is refused. Prove mutation-sensitivity by making the reporting path return success unconditionally and watching the named assertion fire. gofmt, build, vet and the full suite green at the exact head with -count=1; exact clean pushed head with per-path artifacts; independent review. Do not merge. Write no initiative or type tags; proposal 5409bc64 is unratified.","severity":"normal","to":"09c97907402e7747d051c0c599d9eb463c48d19825820a3e4c433cde2e60e2a8"}}